# Draft and lifecycle correction

This is a separate stage at `work/keepass-history-draft-fix-next`. The original 181-artifact history stage, later 546-artifact selection stage and independent 101/36/16/33-artifact reviews remain immutable. Their exact bytes are retained under `preserved-selection-stage/` and `preserved-independent/`; the new candidate does not rebind their old results to its client source.

Independent review caught three further draft boundaries: a completed Restore response replaced Notes entered after the request; a completed Save response cleared newly entered Notes; and Restore accepted an already dirty editor and discarded its unsaved draft. The first two responses came from real staged client/server with an owned DOM model, not an original GUI. The third boundary is also tied to authenticated KeePass 2.61.1 dialog source: Restore is disabled and rechecked when current dialog contents differ from its initial entry. `draft-contract-map.json` binds all three exact source spans. The candidate guard covers the seven editable Title, Group, Username, Password, URL, Notes and TOTP values. It does not claim to execute the complete original form or all original metadata controls.

Controls remain enabled. Save/Restore capture all seven current values, an input revision and a mutation request generation before the first await. Selection/view/session/history guards remain in place. After each response that could redraw the editor, the candidate checks those captures again. Value comparison also covers autofill/programmatic password changes that do not raise an input event; the revision handles a user edit followed by a return to the same values. Private value signatures live in the request closure only and are not recorded by the application.

A completed Save retains a newer draft. When the first Save created an entry, the retained editor adopts that committed ID, so the next Save updates it instead of silently creating another entry. A completed Restore retains a newer draft while refreshing its represented saved baseline from the guarded read; the backend's confirmed restoration already occurred, and the subsequent draft remains unsaved until Save. Restore refuses an editor already different from its loaded baseline before sending any effect request. A delayed Delete retains newly typed values as a New draft without the deleted ID. A delayed generated-password response cannot replace a newer typed password. These are finite candidate transport safeguards, not additional original-runtime comparison counts.

The independent lifecycle probe caught an older detached view's five-minute callback locking an actively reopened session. The same timer pattern appears in the pre-history baseline, so it is separately identified as pre-existing. The stage now returns the cleanup function used by the existing shared host. Cleanup is idempotent: it clears the owned timer, marks the view disposed, invalidates selection/view/session generations, clears detached input values and prevents pending responses from republishing or rearming that view. Cleanup does not issue a server lock; the server's existing session timer remains authoritative. It cannot undo a server effect already dispatched. Tests execute controlled logical callbacks and do not measure five minutes of real time or claim race-free process/browser teardown.

The focused final run is source-bound and preserves raw TAP, exact test/helper sources, original observations and host observations. It includes the original 18 history/HTTP/DOM groups, the prior 10 selection groups, unchanged two pending-draft cases and one dirty-before-Restore case, six adjacent draft groups, the reviewed controlled-timer body with an explicit local helper-import adapter, and three disposal/control groups. The original source study and native history/server/KDBX/XML implementation stay unchanged. Actual original assembly execution remains separate from the added candidate DOM regressions.

Application execution uses no original implementation/runtime. Original release/source/license and historical compatibility evidence retain their exact identities. Browser acceptance and independent post-fix review remain separate evidence; this stage's own DOM model is not a browser engine. Complete product parity, security parity and comparative superiority remain false.
